{"externally_confirmed_facts":["`java.security.KeyPairGenerator` is the Java Cryptography Architecture engine class for generating public/private key pairs for a selected algorithm. Its documented workflow is to obtain an implementation with `getInstance(...)`, optionally initialize it, and generate a pair with `generateKeyPair()` or `genKeyPair()`; the two generation methods are documented as functionally equivalent.","For the reported text `expecting one of RSA,EC,DSA,DiffieHellman,DH but found Ed25519`, the relevant API value is the algorithm name passed as the first argument to `KeyPairGenerator.getInstance(...)`. In the cited CrySL rule, that argument is bound as `algorithm`, and the rule constrains it to `RSA`, `EC`, `DSA`, `DiffieHellman`, or `DH`.","Oracle's Java Security Standard Algorithm Names specification lists `Ed25519` as a `KeyPairGenerator` algorithm. Therefore, the reported allowlist mismatch is not an Oracle JCA statement that `Ed25519` is an invalid `KeyPairGenerator` name; it is a constraint in the cited rule source.","The documented `KeyPairGenerator` usage is to request an implementation using `getInstance` with an algorithm name, optionally selecting a provider; initialize it either with an algorithm-specific integer key size or an `AlgorithmParameterSpec`, optionally with a `SecureRandom`; and call `generateKeyPair()` or `genKeyPair()`. Unsupported integer sizes are documented to cause `InvalidParameterException`, and inappropriate parameter specifications are documented to cause `InvalidAlgorithmParameterException`.","The explicit API guidance relevant to safe, stable use is to initialize explicitly rather than relying on provider defaults: the API says a provider must supply and document defaults when initialization is omitted, but those defaults can vary among providers and can change in future versions. The official API documentation does not recommend replacing `Ed25519` with RSA, EC, DSA, or DH in response to a third-party rule allowlist result.","No initialization argument or runtime diagnostic is supplied. The documentation therefore does not support a conclusion about a particular key-size or parameter-specification error.","For externally encoded keys, the `Key` API documents `getEncoded()` as returning the primary encoded form when one is available and `getFormat()` as identifying the format. It identifies X.509 `SubjectPublicKeyInfo` as a common public-key encoding and PKCS#8 `PrivateKeyInfo` as a common private-key encoding.","RFC 8410 defines algorithm identifiers and ASN.1 encoding usage for Ed25519 public and private keys in X.509/PKIX-related representations. A change between key algorithms consequently requires compatibility consideration by the consuming key format, protocol, certificate profile, keystore, peer, or other consumer; JCA does not define one universal wire protocol for all generated keys.","Java SE 15's Standard Algorithm Names specification lists `Ed25519` as a `KeyPairGenerator` algorithm, and the research evidence states that Oracle JDK 15 implements EdDSA. This does not establish availability for every Java runtime or installed provider configuration; provider support is determined by the target runtime and providers.","No JCA-documented automatic migration or conversion path from an Ed25519 key pair to RSA, EC, DSA, or DH key material was identified. `KeyFactory` documents generation, reconstruction, and translation of keys and key specifications, but is not documented as cross-algorithm key conversion.","The cited rule source declares the governed receiver type as `SPEC java.security.KeyPairGenerator`. Its event clause, quoted verbatim, is:
`EVENTS
	g1: getInstance(algorithm);
	g2: getInstance(algorithm, _);
	Get := g1 | g2;

	i1: initialize(keysize);
	i2: initialize(keysize, _);
	i3: initialize(params);
	i4: initialize(params, _);
	Init := i1 | i2 | i3 | i4;

	k1: keyPair = generateKeyPair();
	k2: keyPair = genKeyPair();
	Gen := k1 | k2;`","According to that rule text, it matches `getInstance` with one or two arguments, `initialize` with one or two arguments, and zero-argument `generateKeyPair` or `genKeyPair`; it syntactically distinguishes those parameter-list shapes. The first `getInstance` argument is `algorithm`; `keysize`, `params`, and `keyPair` are likewise declared values, while `_` denotes an unconstrained second argument in the listed events.","The rule's ordering and initial algorithm condition, quoted verbatim, are:
`ORDER
	Get, Init, Gen
CONSTRAINTS
	algorithm in {"RSA", "EC", "DSA", "DiffieHellman", "DH"}; //BSI TR-02102-1 Recommends atleast 3000bits for keys
	algorithm in {"RSA"} => keysize in {4096, 3072};
	algorithm in {"DSA"} => keysize in {3072};
	algorithm in {"DiffieHellman", "DH"} => keysize in {3072};
	algorithm in {"EC"} => keysize in {256};`","The rule source also declares conditional required predicates for parameter-spec initialization (`preparedRSA`, `preparedDSA`, `preparedDH`, and `preparedEC`) and an ensured `generatedKeypair[keyPair, algorithm]` predicate after compliant use.","Based on the rule text itself, it is not a generic name-only rule: its `SPEC` target is explicitly `java.security.KeyPairGenerator`. The source does not declare matching for a method on an arbitrary receiver merely because that method shares one of these names."],"sources":[{"relevance":"Official API documentation for the class governed by the finding, including factory methods, initialization, generation, exceptions, provider behavior, and explicit-initialization guidance.","limitations":"Documents JCA API semantics, not the behavior of a particular provider, target runtime, key consumer, or detector version.","claim":"`KeyPairGenerator` generates public/private key pairs; documents `getInstance`, `initialize`, `generateKeyPair`, `genKeyPair`, and provider-default behavior.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/security/KeyPairGenerator.html"},{"relevance":"Official specification of standard algorithm names, including the `KeyPairGenerator` name `Ed25519`.","limitations":"Listing a standard name does not guarantee that every installed provider or Java runtime implements it.","claim":"`Ed25519` is a standard `KeyPairGenerator` algorithm name.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/specs/security/standard-names.html"},{"relevance":"Version-specific official algorithm-name specification used for the Java SE 15 platform-support statement.","limitations":"Does not establish support in all Java distributions, provider sets, or earlier platform versions.","claim":"Java SE 15 lists `Ed25519` as a `KeyPairGenerator` algorithm.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/15/docs/specs/security/standard-names.html"},{"relevance":"Official JCA documentation for external key encodings and common key-format examples.","limitations":"Does not define an application-level wire protocol or establish a particular consumer's accepted algorithms.","claim":"`Key.getEncoded()` and `Key.getFormat()` expose an external encoding and its format; X.509 SubjectPublicKeyInfo and PKCS#8 PrivateKeyInfo are common formats.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/security/Key.html"},{"relevance":"Official JCA documentation relevant to the absence of a documented cross-algorithm conversion migration path.","limitations":"Describes `KeyFactory` operations but does not provide migration guidance for an identified application or stored-key population.","claim":"`KeyFactory` supports key generation, reconstruction, and translation; it does not document automatic conversion of Ed25519 keys into RSA, EC, DSA, or DH keys.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/security/KeyFactory.html"},{"relevance":"Primary standards source for Ed25519 algorithm identifiers and ASN.1 encoding in X.509/PKIX-related contexts.","limitations":"Defines relevant interoperable encodings, but not an application's protocol, keystore, certificate policy, or peer support.","claim":"RFC 8410 specifies algorithm identifiers and ASN.1 encoding use for Ed25519 keys in X.509/PKIX-related representations.","authority":"primary","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc8410/"},{"relevance":"Primary source for the cited CrySL rule's `SPEC`, event declarations, ordering, constraints, required predicates, and ensured predicate.","limitations":"The URL follows the repository's moving `master` branch. It does not identify the exact repository commit, rule-pack version, or CryptoAnalysis version that produced the reported result.","claim":"The rule targets `java.security.KeyPairGenerator`, declares the quoted method-event shapes, requires `Get, Init, Gen`, and allowlists only `RSA`, `EC`, `DSA`, `DiffieHellman`, and `DH` for `algorithm`.","authority":"primary","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/KeyPairGenerator.crysl"}],"queries_performed":["Reviewed the supplied research evidence for Oracle `KeyPairGenerator` API documentation.","Reviewed the supplied research evidence for Oracle Java Security Standard Algorithm Names specifications for Java SE 25 and Java SE 15.","Reviewed the supplied research evidence for Oracle `Key` and `KeyFactory` API documentation.","Reviewed the supplied research evidence for RFC 8410.","Reviewed the supplied primary CrySL rule source at https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/KeyPairGenerator.crysl."],"externally_unresolved_questions":["The exact `KeyPairGenerator.getInstance(...)` call, its resolved receiver, selected provider, target Java runtime, and installed provider set are not supplied.","No initialization argument, generated-key encoding, persistence format, certificate profile, protocol, peer capability, keystore, or generated-key consumer is identified. Compatibility of any algorithm change with those consumers cannot be determined from the API and rule documentation alone.","The raw rule URL is on `master`; the exact rule-pack commit, rule-file content hash at scan time, CryptoAnalysis version, and analysis trace that produced the finding are not identified.","For parameter-spec initialization, the rule declares required preparation predicates. Whether a particular parameter object establishes one of those predicates requires the detector version and analysis trace.","The official sources reviewed do not define an automatic cross-algorithm migration path from Ed25519 key material to RSA, EC, DSA, or DH key material."]}
